WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A museum receives three parcels with their labels missing.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:09.600 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One contains a game die, one is a long cereal carton, and
one is a triangular tent model.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.120 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could you identify each object without relying on its colour
or the direction it faces?

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.676 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Let us become geometry detectives.

00:00:17.676 --> 00:00:18.076 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning how to recognise three-dimensional
objects using their faces, edges and vertices.

00:00:18.076 --> 00:00:32.148 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will investigate actual models, connect them to drawings,
and explain why a collection of properties is more reliable
than a quick glance.

00:00:32.148 --> 00:00:39.027 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to identify and describe a solid using
evidence about its faces, edges and vertices.

00:00:39.027 --> 00:00:44.356 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
By the end, you should be able to defend an object label,
rather than simply guess its name.

00:00:44.356 --> 00:00:48.076 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First, I can distinguish a face, an edge and a vertex.

00:00:48.076 --> 00:00:52.515 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Second, I can count these features systematically, including
hidden ones.

00:00:52.515 --> 00:00:57.763 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Third, I can compare two solids and explain which properties
actually distinguish them.

00:00:57.763 --> 00:00:58.283 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about a square drawn on paper and a box you can pick
up.

00:00:58.283 --> 00:01:00.203 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A square is a flat shape.

00:01:00.203 --> 00:01:07.843 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A box is a three-dimensional object: it has length, width
and height.

00:01:07.843 --> 00:01:11.579 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its outside is made from surfaces that surround a space.

00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:14.819 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Find an empty carton or imagine holding one.

00:01:14.819 --> 00:01:16.859 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Slowly turn it in your hands.

00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:22.059 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A drawing only shows one viewpoint, while the object
continues behind the visible parts.

00:01:22.059 --> 00:01:27.571 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We must account for the whole object, including surfaces and
corners we cannot currently see.

00:01:27.571 --> 00:01:31.091 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A flat face is one flat region on the surface.

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:35.171 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Place a paper square against a game die: the square matches
one face.

00:01:35.171 --> 00:01:38.131 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Turn the die and another face comes into view.

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:41.631 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each face belongs to the same solid, even when it is hidden.

00:01:41.631 --> 00:01:44.871 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An edge is a line where two flat faces meet.

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:46.351 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Trace one join on the carton with a finger.

00:01:46.351 --> 00:01:50.311 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The edge is shared by the two faces.

00:01:50.311 --> 00:01:55.649 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Counting it once for each face would count the same edge
twice, so we need a careful method.

00:01:55.649 --> 00:01:58.769 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A vertex is a corner point where edges meet.

00:01:58.769 --> 00:02:01.649 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Touch a corner of your carton with a sticky dot.

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:05.249 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The dot marks a position, rather than a length or an area.

00:02:05.249 --> 00:02:09.756 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The plural of vertex is vertices: one vertex, several
vertices.

00:02:09.756 --> 00:02:11.356 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at the cube model.

00:02:11.356 --> 00:02:14.556 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can see only some of its faces in this drawing.

00:02:14.556 --> 00:02:17.476 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How many faces does the complete cube have?

00:02:17.476 --> 00:02:21.716 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about the face resting on the table and those at the
back.

00:02:21.716 --> 00:02:24.779 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Make your prediction before the answer appears.

00:02:29.779 --> 00:02:31.899 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A cube has six square faces.

00:02:31.899 --> 00:02:35.419 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine top and bottom, front and back, left and right.

00:02:35.419 --> 00:02:38.779 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These three opposite pairs account for every face.

00:02:38.779 --> 00:02:44.740 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Seeing only three regions in a drawing does not mean that
the actual cube has only three faces.

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:47.060 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now organise the cube edges.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:47.620 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
There are four around the top square and four around the
bottom square.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:54.580 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Four more edges join corresponding top and bottom corners.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:58.478 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These are different groups, so no edge has been counted
twice.

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:01.638 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Four plus four plus four equals twelve edges.

00:03:01.638 --> 00:03:05.758 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The calculation records our grouping, rather than replacing
the model.

00:03:05.758 --> 00:03:11.678 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Point to an edge in each group and explain why every cube
edge belongs to exactly one group.

00:03:11.678 --> 00:03:15.117 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That is how a systematic count becomes convincing.

00:03:15.157 --> 00:03:18.557 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For vertices, mark the four corners around the top face.

00:03:18.557 --> 00:03:19.117 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then turn the model and mark four different corners around
the bottom.

00:03:19.117 --> 00:03:20.237 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The joining edges do not introduce any new corners halfway
along them.

00:03:20.237 --> 00:03:29.526 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Four plus four gives eight vertices.

00:03:29.526 --> 00:03:34.206 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our cube record now says six faces, twelve edges and eight
vertices.

00:03:34.206 --> 00:03:35.646 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each number answers a different question.

00:03:35.646 --> 00:03:42.326 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A face is a flat region, an edge is a shared boundary, and a
vertex is a corner point.

00:03:42.326 --> 00:03:45.280 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain the difference using the same object.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:51.160 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A classmate counts four edges around each of six faces and
announces twenty-four cube edges.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.720 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The multiplication counted every shared edge twice.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.120 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Twelve actual edges each belong to two faces.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:03.764 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We should record twelve, because we are counting distinct
edges, not appearances around faces.

00:04:03.764 --> 00:04:06.484 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Consider a long rectangular carton.

00:04:06.484 --> 00:04:10.404 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It also has six faces, twelve edges and eight vertices.

00:04:10.404 --> 00:04:13.484 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Those counts alone do not separate it from a cube.

00:04:13.484 --> 00:04:19.251 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at the face shapes and actual edge lengths: a cube has
six squares and all its edges are equal.

00:04:19.251 --> 00:04:22.291 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A cube is a special rectangular prism.

00:04:22.291 --> 00:04:24.051 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every square face is also a rectangle, because it has four
right angles.

00:04:24.051 --> 00:04:31.091 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A non-cube rectangular prism has some unequal edge lengths.

00:04:31.091 --> 00:04:37.109 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Avoid saying that cubes and rectangular prisms can never
belong to the same mathematical family.

00:04:37.109 --> 00:04:41.149 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Let us investigate a different package: a triangular prism.

00:04:41.149 --> 00:04:47.349 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Make two matching card triangles and join their
corresponding edges using three rectangular strips.

00:04:47.349 --> 00:04:49.749 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The triangular ends stay parallel.

00:04:49.749 --> 00:04:53.069 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The rectangles connect them into one closed solid.

00:04:53.069 --> 00:04:55.997 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict which features will differ from the cube.

00:04:55.997 --> 00:04:58.637 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
There are two triangular end faces.

00:04:58.637 --> 00:05:03.797 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each of the three sides of a triangular end connects to one
rectangular face.

00:05:03.797 --> 00:05:08.197 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two triangles and three rectangles give five faces
altogether.

00:05:08.197 --> 00:05:13.820 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Notice that the word triangular describes the end faces, not
every face on the prism.

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:16.580 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Mark three corners on the front triangle.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.740 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Mark three matching corners on the back triangle.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:23.260 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These six positions are all the vertices of the prism.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:28.355 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A line joining a front corner to a back corner adds an edge,
not another vertex.

00:05:28.355 --> 00:05:28.835 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before counting along with me, predict the number of edges
on the triangular prism.

00:05:28.835 --> 00:05:40.195 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Organise your thinking into the front triangle, the back
triangle, and the joins between corresponding corners.

00:05:40.195 --> 00:05:44.705 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep a total in mind, and check that you have not counted
any join twice.

00:05:49.705 --> 00:05:52.185 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The front triangle has three edges.

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:53.625 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The back triangle has three more.

00:05:53.625 --> 00:05:54.345 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Three joining edges connect the matching corners.

00:05:54.345 --> 00:05:56.185 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Three plus three plus three equals nine.

00:05:56.185 --> 00:06:04.745 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The complete record is five faces, nine edges and six
vertices.

00:06:04.745 --> 00:06:07.631 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each count is supported by a visible grouping.

00:06:07.631 --> 00:06:09.551 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A triangular prism is not a triangular pyramid.

00:06:09.551 --> 00:06:11.151 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A prism has two matching parallel ends joined by side faces.

00:06:11.151 --> 00:06:20.791 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A triangular pyramid has four triangular faces meeting
around four vertices.

00:06:20.791 --> 00:06:26.052 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Saying that an object contains triangles is not enough to
distinguish the two structures.

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:29.812 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A square-based pyramid provides another useful comparison.

00:06:29.812 --> 00:06:31.692 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Start with one square base.

00:06:31.692 --> 00:06:36.372 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Join a triangular face to each of its four sides, bringing
the triangle tips to one apex.

00:06:36.372 --> 00:06:43.209 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One square and four triangles give five faces, just like the
triangular prism’s face count.

00:06:43.209 --> 00:06:44.969 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The square-based pyramid has four base corners and one apex,
so it has five vertices.

00:06:44.969 --> 00:06:47.369 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Four base edges and four rising edges give eight edges.

00:06:47.369 --> 00:07:00.519 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It shares the triangular prism’s five-face count, but the
face shapes, edge count and vertex count reveal different
objects.

00:07:00.519 --> 00:07:04.999 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Not every familiar solid is made entirely of flat polygon
faces.

00:07:04.999 --> 00:07:07.839 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A cylinder has two flat circular faces and a curved surface
connecting them.

00:07:07.839 --> 00:07:07.959 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A sphere has one continuous curved surface.

00:07:07.959 --> 00:07:18.342 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use these properties when a museum object does not resemble
a prism or a pyramid.

00:07:18.342 --> 00:07:20.022 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A circular boundary is not a row of tiny vertices.

00:07:20.022 --> 00:07:25.342 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A cylinder has no corners where straight edges meet.

00:07:25.342 --> 00:07:34.902 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Be clear about the object you are describing and the
convention you are using: today our edge counts for prisms
and pyramids refer to their straight shared face boundaries.

00:07:34.902 --> 00:07:38.222 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The museum finds an object with five faces.

00:07:38.222 --> 00:07:42.742 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One label says triangular prism and another says
square-based pyramid.

00:07:42.742 --> 00:07:45.702 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can the face count alone settle the choice?

00:07:45.702 --> 00:07:52.641 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Decide what extra evidence you would inspect, and prepare a
reason that uses more than just the object’s appearance.

00:07:57.641 --> 00:08:00.761 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Five faces alone cannot settle the label.

00:08:00.761 --> 00:08:06.881 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look for two matching triangular ends and three rectangles
to identify a triangular prism.

00:08:06.881 --> 00:08:13.161 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look for a square base and four triangles meeting at an apex
to identify a square-based pyramid.

00:08:13.161 --> 00:08:16.327 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Edge and vertex counts provide additional checks.

00:08:16.327 --> 00:08:22.407 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Create a small table with columns for object name, face
shapes, number of faces, edges and vertices.

00:08:22.407 --> 00:08:24.767 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Draw a quick model beside each row.

00:08:24.767 --> 00:08:31.821 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If two rows have the same number in one column, compare the
other properties before claiming that the objects are
identical.

00:08:31.821 --> 00:08:36.621 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to our learning goal: identify a solid using evidence
about its features.

00:08:36.621 --> 00:08:42.861 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can now locate a face, trace an edge, mark a vertex and
explain a count that includes hidden parts.

00:08:42.861 --> 00:08:48.591 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one object near you, describe it precisely, and tell
a partner which property was most useful.
